WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.945
The support screen asks for one card's payments.

2
00:00:06.945 --> 00:00:13.046
The payments table has a million rows in 23,818 pages of 8 kilobytes.

3
00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:15.346
Card 4242 has 14 payments.

4
00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:17.468
Which pages are they on?

5
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:23.293
With no index, there's only one way to find out: read every page and test every row.

6
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:33.355
That's 23,818 pages for 14 rows, about a quarter of a second, and it gets slower as the table grows.

7
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.027
An index fixes this.

8
00:00:35.027 --> 00:00:42.272
It's a B+tree: a sorted copy of card_id, where every entry points to the page that holds its row.

9
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:49.684
The search starts at the root and goes down: the root, one inner page, then the leaf that holds 4242.

10
00:00:49.867 --> 00:01:00.028
The leaf lists 14 row addresses, so PostgreSQL reads just those 14 table pages: 17 pages in all, about a quarter of a millisecond.

11
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.020
Now ask for the card's total.

12
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:08.235
If the index also stores the amount, with INCLUDE, the leaf already has everything the query needs.

13
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:10.241
That's an index only scan.

14
00:01:10.241 --> 00:01:18.239
It reads three index pages and never touches the table, as long as VACUUM has marked those table pages all visible.

15
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:20.999
So why not use an index for every query?

16
00:01:20.999 --> 00:01:22.795
Take the succeeded payments.

17
00:01:22.795 --> 00:01:27.285
They're 93 percent of all payments, and they sit on almost every page.

18
00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:37.991
Through the index, that's 930,000 jumps from an index entry to its table page, in no useful order, landing on the same pages again and again.

19
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.454
Reading the table straight through touches each page once, in order.

20
00:01:42.454 --> 00:01:46.896
So the planner picks the sequential scan, even with an index on status.

21
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.644
An index wins when the query needs a short run of entries.

22
00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:53.577
When it needs most of the table, read the table.
